## TL;DR (Abstract)

The challenge presents a storefront program where buying the authentic flag requires funds far exceeding the starting balance. By inspecting the source code, a signed 32-bit integer overflow vulnerability was found in the total cost calculation when ordering fake flags. Supplying a sufficiently large order quantity caused the multiplication arithmetic to wrap into negative numbers, resulting in a negative total cost that increased the balance instead of reducing it, allowing purchase of the real flag.

## Challenge Description

> There's a flag shop selling items, but the real flag is way out of your budget. Can you find a way to afford the real flag?

## Thought Process & Walkthrough

### 1. Initial Reconnaissance

Inspecting the provided source code shows the initial balance and item costs:

* Starting balance: `1100`
* Real flag cost: `100000`
* Fake flag cost: `900` each

In the routine handling fake flag purchases, the cost calculation uses signed 32-bit integer variables:

```c
int number_flags = 0;
fflush(stdin);
scanf("%d", &number_flags);
if (number_flags > 0) {
    int total_cost = 0;
    total_cost = 900 * number_flags;
    if (total_cost <= account_balance) {
        account_balance -= total_cost;
        printf("\nYour new balance is %d\n\n", account_balance);
    }
}
```

### 2. Exploitation via Integer Overflow

A signed 32-bit integer (`int`) has a maximum representation of:

$$2^{31} - 1 = 2,147,483,647$$

When `total_cost = 900 * number_flags` exceeds this maximum boundary, it wraps around to a negative value in two's complement arithmetic.

Entering an order size such as `2386093`:

$$900 \times 2,386,093 = 2,147,483,700$$

Because $2,147,483,700 > 2,147,483,647$, the calculation overflows to $-2,147,483,596$.

Subtracting this negative total cost (`account_balance -= total_cost`) produces an addition:

$$\text{account\_balance} - (-\text{value}) = \text{account\_balance} + \text{value}$$

This increases the account balance into the billions.

